        <div class="cav">The locked drawer</div>
        <h2 id="history">Where do Amex's 6-statement and 2-year limits leave you?</h2>
        <p>They leave a real gap the moment your history runs past two years, and this is the honest core of the page. Amex says "your previous two years of transactions can be viewed electronically" with sorting, filtering, and download to PDF, Excel or CSV, and that older statements are archived and made "accessible online within 24 hours of your request" (<a href="https://www.americanexpress.com/us/customer-service/faq.transaction-billing-statements-history.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">American Express</a>). So an older tax year or a multi-year review means requesting archived PDFs and then turning them into a spreadsheet yourself. Amex also provides a free Year-End Summary that shows spending "by month and spend category" for the previous calendar year, which is a useful overview but a summary, not the line-by-line rows you can re-sort and total (<a href="https://www.americanexpress.com/us/customer-service/faq.year-end-summary-statement.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">American Express</a>). When you need the transactions themselves, from any year, you convert the statements.</p>
        <div class="callout peach"><div class="h">Two windows, not one</div><p>Keep the two limits straight: six billing statements export to Quicken, QuickBooks, or CSV; two years are viewable and exportable as PDF, Excel, or CSV; older than that is a PDF you request. A converter that reads the PDF is what bridges all three back into one spreadsheet.</p></div>

        <div class="cav">Know the page</div>
        <h2 id="structure">What is on an Amex statement, and what comes across?</h2>
        <p>An American Express statement is organized into clear blocks, and one of them is genuinely Amex-specific. Amex's own guide lists the account summary with previous balance, payments and credits, new charges, fees, interest and new balance; the payment information; a rewards balance where it applies; the transaction detail; and a fees-and-interest section with year-to-date totals (<a href="https://www.americanexpress.com/en-us/credit-cards/credit-intel/credit-card-statement/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">American Express</a>). On family and business accounts, Amex groups the new charges by card member, listing each additional card member's charges under their name, which is exactly the kind of detail a converter needs to read into rows so you can tell whose charge is whose. The tool pulls the transaction detail into date, description, and amount and reconciles the sum to the printed total; the summary and interest sections stay on the statement as the source of truth.</p>
